\description{
Final-weight step for rotating panels that exploits the sample overlap \strong{in the
estimation}, not only in the variance. It is the composite calibration of the
Canadian LFS (Gambino, Kennedy and Singh 2001; Fuller and Rao 2001) and the
composite calibration of the Uruguayan ECH (INE, methodology sec. 8.4): the same
estimator. On top of the ordinary calibration to known population totals \code{X}, it
adds a second set of constraints to control totals \code{Zhat} estimated from the
\strong{previous wave} (the composite auxiliaries: previous-month labour status,
optionally crossed by domains). Because \code{Zhat} uses the previous wave's composite
weights, the estimator is recursive.
}
\details{
The composite auxiliary \code{z} is known for the overlap sample (units present in
\code{t-1}) and imputed for the birth rotation group (units entering at \code{t}, with no
\code{t-1} value) by combining two imputations: MR1 (mean imputation, aimed at the
level) and MR2 (carry-backward, aimed at the change), mixed as
\verb{z = (1 - alpha) z1 + alpha z2} with \code{alpha = 2/3}.

The composite weights are \code{w_cre = w_nr * g}, with \code{g} the GREG factor of the
augmented calibration \verb{[x | z]} to \verb{[X | Zhat]}; the same solver as
\code{\link[=step_calibrate]{step_calibrate()}}. With \code{equal_within_cluster = TRUE} the auxiliaries are
averaged to the household (integrated method of weighting, Lemaitre-Dufour 1987)
so every household member shares one final weight.
}
